<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The shader graph: a node library of maths, texture, vertex-data and master nodes over the
            reusable node-graph framework, compiling to Raven source that the same compiler a
            hand-written shader goes through then type-checks.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>editor;shader-graph;node-graph;raven;shaders;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <!--
          CA1051 (no visible instance fields) is the one rule this assembly cannot keep. A node's
          ports *are* its fields — that is the design in docs/plan/11, and it is what lets a node
          read as `[Input] public DynamicVector A;` and an Emit read as `$"{A} + {B}"`. Properties
          would work and would mean the generator wrote a backing store and the author wrote `A.Value`
          everywhere, which is a worse declaration for no benefit: these types are compile-time
          scaffolding built and thrown away by the compiler, never a public API anyone holds.
        -->
        <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1051</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.csproj" />

        <!--
          The preview renderer's three, and nothing else: a device to make a target on, the effect
          model that says what a compiled variant binds, and the in-process Raven compiler the editor
          already resolves its own variants through. The node library and the emitter are still
          reachable with none of them — `ShaderGraphPreview` compiles a sub-expression to text on its
          own, which is what the compiler tests exercise.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Graphics\Vixen.Graphics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Shaders\Vixen.Shaders.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Tools\Vixen.ShaderCompiler\Vixen.ShaderCompiler.csproj" />

        <!--
          And the material model, for the one thing a graph cannot do on its own: say what it is as a
          material. `Master/Surface` emits an `IMaterialSurface`, which is exactly what an
          `IMaterialFeature` names, so turning a compiled graph into the feature a `.vxmat` composes
          is a mechanical read of `ShaderGraphSource` — and it belongs beside the compiler that
          produced those names rather than in whichever assembly happens to import a file. Editor to
          Core, which is the direction CheckArchitecture allows; nothing here opens a device or knows
          about a project.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Rendering\Vixen.Rendering.csproj" />

        <!--
          The node generator, over this assembly's own [Node] classes. Analyzers do not flow through
          a ProjectReference, so every assembly declaring nodes references it directly — which is
          also what a third-party plugin does, and the reason the arrangement is worth keeping plain.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.ShaderGraph.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
